1 The problem
*************

Elpaca installs a package by cloning its source, byte-compiling the
elisp, and generating autoloads.  For most packages that is everything
needed.  For roughly 16% of GNU/NonGNU ELPA packages (and a smaller
slice of MELPA packages), there are additional build steps that upstream
_does_ run — on the ELPA/MELPA build server — but that Elpaca does not
replay locally.

   Concrete examples of what gets lost:

   • AUCTeX ships ‘latex/preview.sty’ and friends only after ‘make elpa’
     runs.  Without that step, the ‘preview-latex’ feature fails with
     ‘File 'preview.sty' not found.’ at LaTeX time.
   • ESS, Eat, Evil, Bongo and dozens of others ship Info manuals that
     only exist after ‘makeinfo’ runs — in some cases with ‘cd’ into a
     ‘doc/’ subdirectory first so ‘@include’ paths resolve, in others
     with a preceding ‘make’ to generate a ‘version.texi’.
   • A handful of packages (Hyperbole, Tramp, flymake-collection, etc.)
     need a ‘make’ target that produces a ‘.el’ file at runtime.

   ‘package.el’ sidesteps all of this by downloading pre-built tarballs
that the upstream build server already assembled.  Elpaca installs from
source, which is more flexible — pin a commit, follow a branch, patch
locally — but means every build quirk would have to be replayed on the
user's machine.  This library does the replay.


