2 How it works
**************

A single hook on ‘elpaca-recipe-functions’.  For each package Elpaca
resolves, the hook branches:

  1. *Metadata-driven path.*  If the package has a ‘:make’, ‘:doc’, or
     ‘:shell-command’ entry in GNU/NonGNU ELPA's ‘elpa-packages’ file
     (fetched on first use and cached locally), synthesize exactly those
     commands:

          :shell-command CMD   -> sh -c CMD   in source dir
          :make TGT            -> make TGT    in source dir
          :doc "D/F.texi"      -> cd D && makeinfo F.texi -o F.info
          :doc "D/F.org"       -> cd D, run Emacs batch with ox-texinfo to export
                                  F.org to .texi (respecting #+export_file_name:
                                  and other org directives), then makeinfo on
                                  the produced file.  Handles the many packages
                                  whose :doc source is a README.org (corfu,
                                  consult, vertico, etc.).

     Then mirror ELPA's "ship everything in source" packaging rule,
     honoring ‘:lisp-dir’, ‘:ignored-files’, and ‘:excludes’ from the
     same entry.

  2. *Heuristic path.*  For MELPA-only packages (or ELPA entries without
     build fields): scan the source tree for ‘.texi’ files, keep ones
     that look like standalone manuals (have ‘@setfilename’ or start
     with ‘\input texinfo’), run ‘makeinfo --force’ on each from its own
     directory.  ‘@include’ fragments (fdl.texi, gpl.texi, etc.)  are
     detected and skipped.  This is a best-effort heuristic — no
     upstream metadata, no guarantees.  It handles the normal case (one
     ‘.texi’ manual, maybe a few fragments, no exotic build pipeline)
     and quietly produces nothing for packages that don't fit.  If a
     MELPA package has a weirder build (texi generated by a Makefile
     target, texi fetched over the network at build time, nested
     manuals, custom makeinfo flags), you'll have to write your own
     ‘:build’ for that one.

  3. *Respect existing ‘:build’.*  Recipes that already set ‘:build’
     (e.g.  ‘elpaca-menu-org’'s custom pipeline for Org) are left alone.

   In both paths the library disables ‘elpaca-build-docs’ (which has
multi-file ‘makeinfo’ bugs) and queues ‘elpaca--install-info’ so the
‘dir’ entry gets updated.


