6 Limitations
*************

   • Packages whose build requires a C compiler plus system libraries
     (pdf-tools' ‘epdfinfo’, vterm's module, irony-mode's server) are
     installed as elisp only, same as under vanilla Elpaca and under
     ‘package.el’.  You'll still need the package's own ‘M-x
     pdf-tools-install’ / equivalent plus the system packages.

   • GNU ELPA ‘:core’ packages (bind-key, eglot, eldoc, erc, …) are
     copies of files inside Emacs's own source tree, not independent
     repos.  There is nothing for Elpaca to clone, so this library can't
     install them from source.  You already have them bundled with Emacs
     anyway; if you specifically want the ELPA-newer version, use ‘:type
     tar :inherit elpaca-menu-gnu-elpa’.


File: elpaca-build-augment.info,  Node: Caveat install-time filesystem scan,  Next: Regenerating the manual,  Prev: Limitations,  Up: Top

7 Caveat: install-time filesystem scan
**************************************

Both paths recursively walk the package's source tree at install time —
the ELPA path to build a flat ‘(source-file . build-file)’ pair list,
the heuristic path to find ‘.texi’ manuals.  The walk is cheap for
normal packages (~10 ms per install) and happens only on
install/rebuild, not on every Emacs startup, but it's unavoidable with
elpaca's current internals: ‘elpaca-generate-autoloads’ and friends skip
symlinked directories, so we can't take the simpler route of ‘:files
("*" (:exclude ...))’ and let elpaca symlink ‘lisp/’ as a whole
directory — nested ‘.el’ files would be invisible to autoload scanning.
We have to symlink files individually.

   If upstream Elpaca ever grew the ability to follow directory symlinks
the walks here could be shrunk or removed entirely.


File: elpaca-build-augment.info,  Node: Regenerating the manual,  Next: License,  Prev: Caveat install-time filesystem scan,  Up: Top

8 Regenerating the manual
*************************

The ‘elpaca-build-augment.info’ file shipped with this repository is
built from ‘README.org’ via ‘ox-texinfo’.  If you change the README, run

     make doc

   and commit the updated ‘elpaca-build-augment.info’ alongside your
README change.  The intermediate ‘.texi’ is ‘.gitignore’'d.  Elpaca's
default ‘:files’ spec picks up ‘*.info’ at the repository root
automatically and its default build pipeline runs
‘elpaca--install-info’, so once the ‘.info’ is present no recipe changes
are needed to ship it.


